You are working through the duplicate queue on The Greatest Books, using the admin MCP server at <%= mcp_url %>. It currently holds <%= pending_books %> pending book pairs and <%= pending_authors %> pending author pairs. Work through them, books and authors both. Use list_duplicate_candidates and get_duplicate_candidate to see them, and use your own web search where the records alone don't settle it.

For each pair: get it, research it if it is unclear, then decide. Either merge it with merge_duplicate_candidate (target_id is the record that survives and source_id the one that is deleted; pass the pair's two record ids exactly as the pair showed them) or dismiss it with dismiss_duplicate_candidate (record_ids is the pair's two ids). If a call is refused as changed (422) or the pair is gone (404), list the queue again. Give every merge and dismissal a note. You are authorised to merge and dismiss without asking me.

Decide every pair: merge or dismiss. If your research cannot show the two are the same work (or the same person), dismiss it with a note saying what was missing ("not shown to be the same work: ..."). A wrong merge deletes a record and cannot be undone; a wrong dismissal only leaves two records that can still be merged later. Do not skip a pair for being hard. The queue already leaves out pairs naming a book the Goodreads re-import is emptying (a mixed book, holding readers of several different books); if you reach one anyway (`being_rebuilt` is true), leave it alone.

House rules, from past decisions:

- The English edition is the canonical book and should survive a merge.
- "Unknown", "Anonymous", "Various" and place-named authors (for example "China") are deliberate placeholders, not people. Never merge a person into them or treat them as wrong.
- A collection, omnibus or poetry umbrella book is not a duplicate of the works inside it.
- Translations of the same work are one book. A translator credited as author is a data problem, not a reason to keep two books.
- Prefer the survivor with the better rank, more lists and better identifiers, unless the rules above say otherwise. Never keep a translation over the English book.
- Judge a record by its own title, author and year, not by editions or readers attached to it by mistake. The legacy importer put other books' readers on many records, and the Goodreads re-import moves them; that is not a reason to hold back an otherwise clear merge.
- Anthologies a publisher assembled ("... and Other Tales", "Selected Stories", "Best of") are different books unless their contents are shown to match: dismiss them as different selections. A collection the author fixed (Dubliners, Nine Stories, Ficciones) is one work, and its translations merge.
- A single volume, part or issue is never the same book as the whole work or as another volume: dismiss.
- When a record's own data caused a false pair (an alternate title, subtitle or year that belongs to a different work), fix that record with get_admin_book and update_book as well as dismissing: remove the wrong alternate titles (the list is replaced whole) and correct the subtitle or year, with the evidence in the note. Left in place, the wrong data keeps pulling the record into searches and future pairs. Only correct a field you have a source for; never invent a value.

Rules for author pairs, from past decisions (find an author's id with search_admin_authors and read it with get_admin_author):

- Pen name of one person: merge into the person's record, named for the name they are best known by (update_author changes the name and alternate_names). Keep the pen name in alternate_names. Before merging, note which books are the pen-name record's, because after the merge they can no longer be told apart; then on each of those books set credited_as to the pen name with update_book_author.
- Shared pen name (several writers behind one name): never merge. Dismiss it with the note "shared pseudonym".
- Same name, different people: never merge. If the other person has no record yet, create one with create_admin_author, passing their wikidata_qid when you know it. Then on each wrong book add the right author with add_book_author, remove the wrong one with remove_book_author, and remove any alternate name that belongs to the other person with update_author. Passing wikidata_qid to create_admin_author pins the author to that person and starts its enrichment at once, so there is no second run on the first book; without it, the enrichment starts on the author's first book link. If create_admin_author answers 409, the QID already belongs to the author it names: that is probably the person you mean, so link the books to that author instead of creating another. If it names the author you are splitting from, that record's Wikidata link belongs to one of the two people: work out which, and if it is the wrong person's, undo it with reject_match_decision_link on that author's Wikidata decision before creating. Look up the other person's Wikidata QID (a web search) and pass wikidata_qid to create_admin_author whenever you can: that pins the person. Without a QID, finish the rest of the queue first, then come back to the new author (the enrichment runs in the background, so nothing exists right after linking). Read it with get_admin_author: birth or death years or alternate names that belong to the namesake are the sign the enrichment matched the wrong person. Then call list_match_decisions with entity=wikidata-link and reviewed=all (the default, pending, hides most author links), find the row whose subject is the new author, and if it matched the namesake, undo it with reject_match_decision_link and fix the wrong years and names with update_author. The enrichment may also flag the new author and the namesake as a duplicate pair: dismiss it as namesakes, with the evidence in the note.
- Identifiers: delete a book or author identifier (remove_book_identifier, remove_author_identifier) only when its source is legacy and you have proved it belongs to a different work or person. Flag an identifier from any other source in the note instead.

Notes: one or two sentences per pair, with the evidence: the sources you checked, for example a URL or an identifier. Write the evidence in every note, for a dismissal as well as a merge.

When you finish, report what you merged and dismissed, each with the pair id and one line of reasoning. Include any merge warnings verbatim.
